 "John VI de Vendôme, Count of Vendôme and Castres (1354–1365) was a member of the House of Montoire and was the son of Bouchard VI (1290–1354) and Alix de Bretagne (1297–1377).", "newValue": "John VI, Count of Vendôme and Castres, reigned from 1354 until his death on February 1, 1364, inheriting his titles amidst the tumultuous early phases of the Hundred Years' War. A scion of the House of Montoire, his countship was defined by the relentless struggle between the English and French crowns, which frequently turned his domains into battlegrounds. His political and military actions, though often reactive, underscore the immense pressures placed upon French nobility during a period of profound national instability and foreign invasion.

His tenure as Count saw him navigating the complex feudal loyalties and military exigencies of a fragmented France. John VI participated in several campaigns against English forces, notably during the aftermath of the disastrous Battle of Poitiers in 1356, which saw King John II of France captured. As a vassal of the French crown, he was compelled to contribute troops and resources, often at great cost to his own territories and subjects. His administrative efforts were largely focused on maintaining order and defending his lands in the face of widespread brigandage and economic disruption exacerbated by the ongoing conflict and the ravages of the Black Death.

